The faer sparse direct solver behind the SPOOLES interface of CalculiX 2.23 (GPL-2.0, like CalculiX itself; a change of the solver logic of CalculiX
made by FEA Studio, 23. 11. 2026). Applied by installer/windows/build_ccx.sh and installer/linux/build_ccx.sh with `patch -p1` in the folder that contains
CalculiX/ccx_2.23/, AFTER ccx_2.23_spooles_serial_solve.patch (the context of the hunks is the source after that patch). The same file lies in
installer/windows/patches/ and installer/linux/patches/. The two new files faer.c and faer.h are not part of this patch: the build scripts copy them from
installer/faer_ccx/ccx/ into CalculiX/ccx_2.23/src/ (they carry their own GPL-2.0 notice and the date), and compile the program with -DFEA_FAER=1, the include
folder of installer/faer_ccx/include/fea_faer.h and the static library libfea_faer.a (the Rust crate installer/faer_ccx: a C interface over faer 0.24.4, MIT).
The text above the first hunk header is ignored by patch(1); only the hunks (from the first minus-minus-minus line on) are the same for the Windows and the Linux build.

What the hunks do: spooles_factor first offers the system of equations to faerc_try_factor (faer.c): a symmetric matrix (inputformat 0) is factorized by faer's
supernodal Cholesky LL^T (a matrix that is not positive definite: faer's Bunch-Kaufman LBL^T inside the supernodes) with the fill-reducing nested dissection of
SPOOLES (the same call as in spooles.c) as the ordering, an unsymmetric matrix (inputformats 1, 3, 4) by faer's supernodal LU with partial pivoting. Every
factorization is CHECKED: one test solve with a pseudo random right hand side and the row-wise backward error of the matrix that CalculiX handed over
(limit 1e-12, like the check of the multi-threaded SPOOLES factorization); a failed factorization or a failed check (and everything faer.c does not take: the dense
matrices of the radiation, small systems, FEA_FAER=0) leaves the system to SPOOLES exactly as before, with a line in the log. spooles_solve and spooles_cleanup follow
the solver that factorized. The symbolic analysis is kept between the factorizations of a matrix with the same pattern. `ccx -v` prints the marker FEA_FAER=1.
Change of 24. 11. 2026 (review fixes): the context of the first hunk follows the marker FEA_SPOOLES_FACTOR_CHECK=2 of ccx_2.23_spooles_serial_solve.patch and the line
numbers of the hunks follow that patch; faer.c (a separate file, not part of this patch) now remembers a decline of the condition guard per pattern, checks the column
counts before it hashes the pattern, compares nzs on reuse and has the limit 0x7fffff00 for the stored entries; the Rust library guards every export against a panic.

